.TH "SKARN-DOCTOR" "1" "2026-06-23" "skarn 0.17.0" "Skarn Manual"
.SH NAME
skarn\-doctor \- Check whether skarn is actually protecting this machine:
binary, license, wired agent hooks, guard log, session stores, and the guard
self\-test
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBskarn\fR \fBdoctor\fR [options]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Check whether skarn is actually protecting this machine: binary, license,
wired agent hooks, guard log, session stores, and the guard self\-test.
.PP
Health, wiring, and dead\-hook detection. Doctor runs one named check per line
\- the binary and its path, the license, each detected agent's hook config,
whether each wired hook command still resolves to an executable, whether the
wiring matches what this version of skarn writes, the guard log's freshness,
the session stores it can enumerate, the guard self\-test, and the cache
directory \- and every warning or failure names the exact command that clears
it. Check ids (binary, license, agent_config_<agent>, guard_command_<agent>,
config_drift_<agent>, guard_log_<agent>, self_test_<agent>, session_stores,
cache_dir) are a stable, greppable contract for fleet scripts. A failure exits
1; a warning never does; \-\-json is a data mode and always exits 0. Doctor is
hermetic: it makes no network call, shells out to nothing, and enumerates the
session stores without reading a session's content.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Add detail to every check: the paths searched, each wired hook command, the
per\-tool session counts, and the resolved Homebrew path.
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Emit the full structured result: {version, checks[{id, status, detail, fix,
notes}], summary{pass, warn, fail}}. Always exits 0.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
skarn(1)
